<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
Copyright 2026 Zachary Olkin. All rights reserved.

Cyclone DDS discovery over a link that will not carry multicast.

ONE BOX, TWO LINKS: put BOTH in the live file
=============================================
A box that is sometimes on the robot LAN and sometimes on a private cable to another
dev machine needs both addresses in `cyclonedds.xml`, not one address that gets edited:

    <NetworkInterface address="192.168.123.220" priority="10" multicast="default" presence_required="false" />
    <NetworkInterface address="192.168.50.20"   priority="0"  multicast="default" presence_required="false" />

`presence_required="false"` downgrades an absent address from FATAL to a warning, and the
priority decides which link wins when both are up. Without it, a pinned address that this box
does not currently have kills the domain and EVERY ros node dies at
`rmw_create_node: failed to create domain`, with the actual reason ("does not match an
available interface") one line above it. This exact stale pin blocked the first hardware
bring-up: the file still named the other machine's cable weeks after the robot arrived, and
because it is gitignored per box, nothing in the repo could notice.

AllowMulticast is a PER LINK decision, and the file only has one
================================================================
`false` is right for a private cable and for campus or guest wifi, which routinely drop
multicast between clients. It is WRONG if any peer on any of the listed links relies on
multicast: a peer that keeps Cyclone's default ephemeral participant ports cannot be reached
by a portless `<Peer>` entry at all (unicast discovery resolves those by probing participant
INDEXES, which `ParticipantIndex auto` is what grants). The two sides then never see each
other while each looks perfectly healthy alone. Multicast on plus the peer list below is the
safe combination when the links differ.

Copy to `cyclonedds.xml` in this directory, edit the peer addresses, and point the container
at it from docker/.env:

    CYCLONEDDS_URI=file:///home/zolkin/sprout/transfer/obelisk/docker/cyclonedds.xml

then recreate the container: compose reads .env only when it CREATES one, so `docker compose
up -d` on its own is not enough and the force recreate flag is required. The path is the one
INSIDE the container, which is the same as the host path, since the whole repo is bind
mounted at $REPO_ROOT. Verify with `scripts/net_check.sh`, whose section 2 prints the parsed
peer list.

NO DOUBLE HYPHEN ANYWHERE IN A COMMENT IN THIS FILE. A double hyphen is illegal inside an
XML comment, and an earlier version of this file was full of them: an underline rule, a
command flag, and several prose dashes. Cyclone's own parser happens to tolerate them, which
is why it went unnoticed, but ElementTree and every other conforming reader rejects the file
outright. It is the same trap this repo has already hit in two MJCF comments and in a
package.xml, so it is called out here rather than left to be rediscovered.

REQUIRED HOST SETTING, ON BOTH BOXES
====================================
SocketReceiveBufferSize below asks for 10 MB, and a kernel that cannot give it is FATAL
rather than a warning: the domain is never created, and every ROS node in the container dies
at startup with

    rmw_create_node: failed to create domain, error Error

preceded by one line naming the real cause, "failed to increase socket receive buffer size
to at least 10485760 bytes, current is 425984 bytes". Ubuntu ships net.core.rmem_max =
212992 (the kernel then offers twice rmem_default), so this fails on a stock box until it is
raised:

    sudo sysctl -w net.core.rmem_max=16777216
    echo 'net.core.rmem_max=16777216' | sudo tee /etc/sysctl.d/60-cyclonedds.conf

The container runs with host networking, so the HOST value is the one that counts, and this
has to be done on the brain box and the world box alike.

WHEN YOU NEED THIS FILE
=======================
Cyclone's default discovery is UDP multicast, and it works on a wired switch with no
configuration at all, which is why nothing in this repo sets CYCLONEDDS_URI and why the
Jetson depth camera has always reached the control stack without one. Reach for this file
only when discovery does not cross:

  * WIFI. Campus, guest and most enterprise APs enable client isolation, which drops
    multicast between clients. Two boxes on the same SSID then see nothing of each other
    while both look perfectly healthy locally.
  * A MULTI HOMED BOX. With several interfaces up, Cyclone picks one for discovery and it
    is not necessarily the one that reaches the other machine. NetworkInterface below
    settles it. This applies even on a clean wired link, because the box still has wifi up
    for internet.
  * A ROUTED LINK. Multicast does not cross subnets without the switch being configured
    for it.

`obk` sets RMW_IMPLEMENTATION and ROS_DOMAIN_ID and does NOT touch CYCLONEDDS_URI, so this
composes with it rather than fighting it.

BOTH BOXES NEED A COPY, each listing the OTHER. A peer list is not symmetric by magic; one
sided discovery gives you a graph where one box sees the other's topics and not the reverse,
which reads as a QoS or type problem and is not one.
-->
<CycloneDDS xmlns="https://cdds.io/config">
  <Domain id="any">
    <General>
      <!-- Which interface to speak on. Autodetermine lets Cyclone choose, which is wrong on
           a box with wifi, wired and docker bridges all up. Pin it: on the Thor the wifi is
           wlP1p1s0 and the wired ports are mgbe0_0 through mgbe0_2 (`ip -brief addr` to
           check). An IP ADDRESS is preferred over a name and is what the committed
           cyclonedds.xml uses: a driver assigned name changes when the adapter does, and on
           a private link the address is the thing you chose deliberately.

             <NetworkInterface address="192.168.50.10" priority="default" multicast="default" />
        -->
      <Interfaces>
        <NetworkInterface autodetermine="true" priority="default" multicast="default" />
      </Interfaces>
      <!-- Off, so discovery uses ONLY the peers below. Leaving it on is harmless where
           multicast works and is exactly what fails silently where it does not. -->
      <AllowMulticast>false</AllowMulticast>
    </General>

    <Discovery>
      <!-- The OTHER machine(s). Unicast, so no multicast is involved at any stage.
           An address with no port reaches every participant index on that host, which is
           what you want: the two halves have several nodes each.

           Fill these in. The brain box lists the world box and vice versa. Localhost stays
           in the list so the local half still finds itself when everything runs on one box
           and this file is left configured. -->
      <Peers>
        <Peer address="localhost" />
        <!-- <Peer address="192.168.50.20" />  the Thor (brain) -->
        <!-- <Peer address="192.168.50.10" />  the dev desktop (world) -->
      </Peers>
      <ParticipantIndex>auto</ParticipantIndex>
    </Discovery>

    <Internal>
      <!-- The 500 Hz encoder and 1 kHz IMU streams are the only ones that stress the link.
           Cyclone's default socket buffers are sized for far less, and the symptom of an
           undersized receive buffer is not an error: it is silently dropped samples that
           read as a jittery estimator.

           SocketReceiveBufferSize[@min], NOT MinimumSocketReceiveBufferSize. The latter is
           what this file shipped with and it is a MOVED setting on this Cyclone pin, which
           logs "setting moved to ..." on every single launch.

           The unit spelling is picky: "kB" and "MB" parse, "KB" does not, and an unparsable
           value is fatal exactly like an unsatisfiable one. See the host setting note at the
           top before assuming 10 MB is free. -->
      <SocketReceiveBufferSize min="10 MB" />
    </Internal>
  </Domain>
</CycloneDDS>
